Number = {75},
Pages = {69-76},
Note = {International Symposium on Human Exposure to Pesticide Residues, Natural
   Toxins and GMOs, BRIGHTON, ENGLAND, NOV   13, 2000},
Abstract = {This paper explores the causes of the current crisis of public
   confidence in genetically modified (GM) crops and the important
   characteristics of the resultant public debate, particularly the need to
   distinguish between arguments based on self interest which will be
   amenable to persuasion based on scientific evidence and those based on
   fundamental Values which will not. Two particularly important components
   of the risk assessment process for GM crops are the concept of
   substantial equivalence in regulating food-related risks, and farm-level
   crop trials to ensure protection of the environment. While it makes
   sense from a regulatory perspective to deal separately with food-related
   and environmental hazards, from the perspective of public perception and
   communication the two aspects are intimately connected. The conclusion
   outlines some important elements of a strategy for progressing the
   public debate beyond the current impasse.},
