Title = {Development of architectural design parameters for energy conservation
   and sustainability in urban mass housing},
Booktitle = {XXX IAHS WORLD CONGRESS ON HOUSING, HOUSING CONSTRUCTION: AN
   INTERDISCIPLINARY TASK, VOLS 1-3},
Year = {2002},
Pages = {975-982},
Note = {30th IAHS World Congress on Housing, UNIV COIMBRA, COIMBRA, PORTUGAL,
   SEP 09-13, 2002},
Organization = {Int Assoc Housing Sci},
Abstract = {The present global housing shortage, which is around 2 billion houses,
   definitely advocates the need of mass housing projects. This shortage
   will further increase by the year 2025, when the world population will
   be around 8.5 billion. But what about the tremendous impact on the field
   of energy usage of these future projects of mass housing?
   The present unplanned and uncontrolled growth of housing cares little
   about energy conservation aspects. Studies reveal that around 18\% of
   total energy consumption of mankind is in housing sector. It is
   necessary to consider energy conservation techniques before, during and
   after construction; as energy can be saved considerably in each stage.
   Over 80\% of the embodied energy in housing is the energy required to
   manufacture the materials. Most of this energy usage is for
   manufacturing only a small number of high-energy materials used in
   construction of housing units, principally steel products, concrete
   products, and ceramic materials. This embodied energy amounts to several
   times the annual energy consumption of that housing in use. Energy is
   used wastefully in heat recovery processes, simple orientation concerns,
   etc. Architectural lighting \& space heating/ cooling are also the
   largest and most visible consumers of energy {[}3]. A properly designed
   energy efficient housing will have a lower initial cost than one planned
   disregarding energy consequences. This advantage derives mainly from
   smaller building volume \& lower energy demands. Architects have the
   opportunity to make a major contribution to the reduction of total
   energy use in built environment through strategies identified.},
