   SAVANNAH, GA, MAR 22-25, 1995},
Organization = {SE Psychol Assoc},
Abstract = {This study investigated differences between men and women in their use
   of sexual stereotypes when evaluating recent hirees. Participants (33
   Euro-American undergraduate women and 45 Euro-American undergraduate
   men) read a job announcement for Personnel Analyst and a completed
   employment application form (with a picture of the applicant attached).
   They then completed a questionnaire, indicating their perceptions of the
   applicant's competence, qualifications, and status on other job-related
   characteristics. The information on the application form was the same
   except for the race and sex of the hiree. Three of the five expected
   interactions mere statistically significant. It appears that men may
   underestimate the qualifications of women and rate them lower than men
   on other job-related characteristics.},
