Note = {XIIIth Annual International Occupational Ergonomics and Safety
   Conference, YPSILANTI, MI, JUN 11-14, 1998},
Abstract = {The present research examiner; older adults' perceptions of the
   readability of OTC medication labels. Twelve labels depicting an OTC
   medication with a fictious name were constructed anti attached to
   bottles. The labels varied in (a) print size (4 point, 7 point, 10
   point), (b) amount of white space between lines/sections of text (no
   line spaces; line spaces between main sections; line spaces between
   separate statements), and (c) label design (standard vs.
   extended/pull-out). Fifty-three older adults rank-ordered the labels
   according to overall ease of reading. In general, participants preferred
   the labels printed with larger type and white space. However, white
   space appears to be less important than print size in readability
   perceptions. Readability perceptions were also higher with the
   alternative extended label designs (Dull-out). Implications for labeling
   on containers with small surface areas is discussed.},
