Organization = {Int Res Inst Stavanger; ESRA Norway; Statoil; Proactima/HSE Acad;
   Safetec; Conocophillips; DNV},
Abstract = {Regulatory response is the preferred approach in the event of a maritime
   disaster. The International Maritime Organization (IMO) has been
   striving to help member governments implement, and more importantly,
   enforce its treaty instruments. It established the sub-committee on Flag
   State Implementation (FSI) in 1992. Member governments were subsequently
   urged to participate in a self assessment of their performance to aid
   identification of weaknesses in the discharge of their responsibilities
   as a flag state. The recently underway Voluntary IMO Member State Audit
   Scheme (VIMSAS) is yet another step. Despite all these measures, the IMO
   continues to be seized with a host of flag states that are perpetual
   stragglers when it comes to giving effect to the conventions. This paper
   looks at the empirical assessment of the public consumption of the media
   to analyze the basis of their perceptions and ascertain who said what,
   to whom, and with what effect. This determination is then applied to
   explore the possibility of optimizing people's risk perception to
   achieve effective implementation of maritime safety legislation.},
